      "title": "PHAR2001_1_4_3_Understanding_Proton_Pump_Enzymatic_Inhibitors",
      "library": "H5P.QuestionSet 1.21",
      "params": {
        "introPage": {
          "showIntroPage": false,
          "startButtonText": "Start Quiz",
          "introduction": ""
        },
        "progressType": "dots",
        "passPercentage": 50,
        "questions": [
          {
            "params": {
              "media": {
                "disableImageZooming": false
              },
              "answers": [
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This answer should not have been selected. Enteric coating simply protects the drug from the stomach acids (or protects the stomach from the damaging effects of the drug). It does not extend the half-life of the drug or make it last longer. A controlled release tablet can delay the absorption of the medication, but neither of these drugs are provided in that way.&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>Well done,&nbsp;Enteric coating simply protects the drug from the stomach acids (or protects the stomach from the damaging effects of the drug). It does not extend the half-life of the drug or make it last longer. A controlled release tablet can delay the absorption of the medication, but neither of these drugs are provided in that way.&nbsp;</div>\n"
                  },
                  "text": "Omeprazole is enteric coated which makes the drug last longer in the system\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "Simplistically speaking, Omeprazole effectively stops the energy supply to the hydrogen potassium pump, while Famotidine is just a competitive antagonist, simply competing with Histamine 2, which is needed for the hydrogen-potassium pump to pump. \n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "<div>Omeprazole is an irreversible inhibitor of the Hydrogen potassium ATPase pump, and since that is such an elaborate protein/pump, it takes quite a while for the parietal cells to either degrade the drug in the parietal cell, or to produce more of that enzymatic pump.</div>\n"
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 100
                }
