              },
              "question": "<p>Omeprazole and other proton pump inhibitors (the …<strong>oprazole</strong> drugs) are much more effective at reducing the acid in the stomach than Famotidine and other Histamine 2 receptor blockers (the …tidine drugs). The explanation for this would include which of the following?</p>\n"
            },
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "metadata": {
              "contentType": "Multiple Choice",
              "license": "U",
              "title": "Untitled Multiple Choice"
            },
            "subContentId": "1717efa6-a7b0-407e-897d-fa80816cfb68"
          },
          {
            "params": {
              "media": {
                "disableImageZooming": false
              },
              "answers": [
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "It is an enzyme because it catalyses a reaction, \n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "It is a protein that is embedded in the parietal cells of the stomach\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  },
                  "text": "It is a protein that acts as a pump, pumping out hydrogen ions into the lumen of the stomach\n"
                },
                {
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>No, the Hydrogen/Potassium ATPase pump is only found in the parietal cells and a few cells in the kidneys. That is why the proton pump inhibitors are fairly well tolerated.&nbsp;</div>\n",
                    "tip": "",
                    "notChosenFeedback": "<div>Yes,&nbsp;the Hydrogen/Potassium ATPase pump is only found in the parietal cells and a few cells in the kidneys. That is why the proton pump inhibitors are fairly well tolerated.</div>\n"
                  },
                  "text": "It is found throughout the body \n"
                }
              ],
